This project investigates a systematic mean-reversion trading strategy using historical financial market data and Python-based quantitative analysis. 
The primary objective was to explore whether deviations from rolling statistical baselines could be used to generate trading signals and whether those signals exhibited 
predictive characteristics under varying market conditions. The strategy employs rolling z-score analysis to identify statistically significant price deviations from a 
moving average. Trading signals are generated when prices move beyond predefined z-score thresholds, under the assumption that extreme deviations may subsequently revert 
toward their historical mean. The research framework includes exploratory backtesting, parameter sensitivity analysis, volatility-regime analysis, Monte Carlo simulation, 
and robustness testing across multiple assets and market environments. Although the project was developed as a research-oriented simulation rather than a production trading
system, it provides a practical framework for evaluating systematic trading concepts and demonstrates core quantitative finance methodologies.
